<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We All Do Chores – Lesson # 1</h2>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 20px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We All Do Chores.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions on familiar topics. Interactive: Engage in role-plays based on familiar social situations.</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions about household chores (e.g., "What do you do at home?" "I wash the dishes every day."). Can engage in role-plays based on familiar social situations involving household responsibilities (e.g., "I clean my room on Saturdays."). Can summarize key points about daily chores from discussions (e.g., "Everyone has chores at home.").</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 25%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and shows a set of flashcards depicting various household chores (e.g., <i>wash the dishes, sweep the floor, clean the bedroom, cook, help, dust, mop, tidy up</i>). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and "Do you help at home?" Students respond with simple "yes" or "no" or by pointing. This activates prior knowledge related to the theme "We All Do Chores."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards one by one, clearly stating the chore (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "clean the bedroom"). The teacher models the pronunciation, exaggerating the /w/ sound in "wash" and the /s/ sound in "sweep." The teacher also claps syllables for words like "cook" (closed) and "do" (open) to introduce phonemic awareness. The teacher performs simple TPR actions for each chore to clarify meaning.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using concept-checking questions.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "wash the dishes": "Do I clean plates or clothes?" (Expected answer: Plates!) "Do I use water?" (Expected answer: Yes!).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For "sweep the floor": "Do I use a broom or a sponge?" (Expected answer: Broom!). "Do I clean the floor or the table?" (Expected answer: The floor!).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (20-30 seconds) between two children, "Ana" and "Tom," discussing their daily chores at home. The dialogue uses simple present tense and introduces vocabulary like <i>dishes, floor, kitchen, bedroom, chores, wash, clean, sweep</i>, and the phrase "every day."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of the main points of extended discussions on familiar topics.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Introduce the audio: "Now, we will listen to Ana and Tom. They are talking about helping at home."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Play the audio once for gist comprehension.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist questions):</b> Ask: "Who is talking?" (Ana and Tom) "What are they talking about?" (Chores / helping at home).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Play the audio a second time.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (True/False):</b> Write simple True/False statements on the board or project them. Students listen and say "True" or "False."
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana washes the dishes. (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tom sweeps the kitchen. (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They help their family every day. (True)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension for present simple sentences, WH-questions ("What do you do?"), frequency adverbs ("every day"), and target vocabulary (<i>wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, dishes, floor, kitchen, bedroom</i>). Pronunciation focus on /w/ and /s/ sounds.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Listening Gap-fill):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences from the audio dialogue, but with key vocabulary words missing (e.g., "Ana _______ the dishes." "Tom _______ the floor."). Students listen to the audio again (or a slower, re-recorded version) and fill in the blanks. Teacher checks answers orally.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Listen and Choose):</b> The teacher says a sentence, and students choose between two pictures or written words. For example, the teacher says, "I wash the dishes," and students point to a picture of washing dishes or a picture of cleaning a room. Or, the teacher says "sweep" and students choose between "sweep" and "sleep" (focus on minimal pairs or similar sounds for listening discrimination, especially for /s/ and /w/ sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Listening & Repeat):</b> In pairs, students take turns saying one of the chore sentences (e.g., "I wash the dishes every day."). Their partner listens and repeats the sentence, paying attention to the pronunciation of /w/ and /s/. The teacher circulates, offering corrective feedback on pronunciation and grammar.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Short guided role-play in pairs.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Divide students into pairs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provide a simple dialogue frame on the board:
                            <br>A: "What do you do at home?"
                            <br>B: "I _______ the _______ every day." / "I _______ my _______ on Saturdays."
                            <br>A: "Oh, you help your family!"
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice using the target vocabulary (<i>wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, dishes, floor, kitchen, bedroom</i>) and grammar (present simple, "every day") to engage in a short conversation about their chores. Emphasize listening to their partner's response.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors and encourages communication, focusing on fluency and task completion rather than strict accuracy.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play and a final listening task.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher asks a few individual students "What do you do at home?" and listens for their response using learned vocabulary. Alternatively, the teacher plays a short, new audio clip about a child doing a chore and asks students to identify the chore they heard.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /w/ and /s/ sounds in target words.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>wash, dishes, sweep, floor</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of answer when asked "What do you do at home?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates basic comprehension of simple chore descriptions.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with these prompts:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can understand words about chores. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can understand sentences about chores. (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new words about chores did you learn today?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about listening to the conversations?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The flashcards and TPR were effective for introducing new vocabulary and concepts. Students showed good initial comprehension of gist information but some struggled with listening for specific details in longer sentences. The pronunciation practice for /w/ and /s/ was helpful. For the next lesson, I need to provide more scaffolded listening tasks with visual support to aid in comprehension of specific information. I also need to reinforce the "every day" adverb more explicitly.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter the same vocabulary (<i>wash, clean, sweep, dishes, floor, kitchen, bedroom, every day</i>) in a short reading text about family chores. This will build on their listening comprehension from today and allow them to see the words in written form.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>